UID:lanesboro-arts-bipoc-artist-residency-2026-2027@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211015Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: Lanesboro Arts BIPOC Artist Residency 2026-2027
DESCRIPTION:Residency for Minnesota-based BIPOC artists (Native American/Am
 erican Indian/Native Alaskan\, Asian\, Black/African American\, Hispanic/L
 atino/Chicano/Latinx\, Middle Eastern/North African\, Native Hawaiian/Paci
 fic Islander\, SWANA) and their families\, run by Lanesboro Arts in rural 
 southeastern MN. NO OUTPUT OR PRODUCT REQUIRED: the residency provides wha
 tever the artist needs (restoration\, family getaway\, connection to natur
 e\, access to rural community\, artistic practice) within the context of t
 he community\; only one community share-back event is required (workshop\,
  school visit\, small gathering\, or blog post\; arranged with Lanesboro A
 rts staff after selection). HOUSING: private bedroom + bath in either the 
 St. Mane Theatre Artist Residency Center (sleeps 1-4) or the Art Loft abov
 e the Gallery (sleeps 1-5)\; kitchenette/full kitchen\, studio space\, lau
 ndry\, Wi-Fi\; neither space is currently ADA-accessible. COHORT OPTION: s
 olo\, OR apply with a specific BIPOC artist collaborator\, OR apply open t
 o being matched with another BIPOC resident at the same time (both receive
  the stipend\; lodging split between St. Mane and the Art Loft). Spouses/p
 artners only get a stipend if they themselves are applying as artists in t
 he cohort model. SELECTION: lottery process (no traditional artistic-merit
  gatekeeping)\; 6-12 residents chosen depending on session lengths\; lotte
 ry is recorded and shared with applicants for transparency. ELIGIBILITY: B
 IPOC artists currently based in Minnesota. APPLY via the Lanesboro Arts we
 bsite. TIMELINE: initial deadline 11:59pm Monday 10 August 2026\; lottery 
 the week of 10 August 2026\; rolling applications thereafter if slots rema
 in\, until 1 April 2027 or until full. Lanesboro Liaison (staff or vetted 
 community member) optionally available for tours\, grocery delivery\, and 
 outdoor recreation (biking\, kayaking\, hiking\, state parks\, etc.).\n\nO
 rganisation: Lanesboro Arts\nLocation: Lanesboro\, Minnesota\, United Stat
 es\nAward: Weekly stipend of $1\,250 for food\, mileage and other costs wh
 ile in residence (half paid two weeks before residency\, half on the final
  day). Additional budget up to $500 per residency for art supplies if a cr
 eative project is pursued. Free private housing (St. Mane Theatre apartmen
 t or Art Loft above the gallery) plus access to studio space\, kitchen\, l
 aundry\, Wi-Fi\, and an optional Lanesboro Liaison for community/outdoor-r
 ecreation connections. No application fee.\nDuration: 1 or 2 weeks\; resid
 encies take place between September 2026 and April 2027\; 8 total weeks di
 stributed across 6-12 residents\n\nMore: https://lanesboroarts.org/bipoc-a
 rtist-residency/application/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
URL:https://lanesboroarts.org/bipoc-artist-residency/application/?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Lanesboro\, Minnesota\, United States
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pioneer-works-visual-arts-music-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211015Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260717
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260718
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pioneer Works: 2027 Open Call – Visual Arts and Music R
 esidency (Brooklyn\, NY)
DESCRIPTION:Pioneer Works in Red Hook\, Brooklyn invites applications from 
 emerging visual artists and musicians for its 2027 residency cohort. 12 Vi
 sual Arts residents (5-month residency) and 12 Music residents (1-month re
 sidency) are selected each year through a two-phase jury process. WHAT IS 
 PROVIDED: free studio space\; access to facilities including risograph pri
 nter\, darkroom\, ceramics studio\, large-format printer\, 3D printers\, t
 hermobinder\, and laser cutter\; biweekly studio visits with curators\, ga
 llerists\, critics\, and artists\; free access to all public programmes at
  Pioneer Works\, including Second Sundays (monthly open studios and perfor
 mances). FINANCIAL AWARD: USD 5\,000 for Visual Arts residents\; USD 3\,00
 0 for Music residents (each comprising a USD 2\,500 honorarium plus USD 50
 0/month for the residency duration)\; applicants' choice on how to spend i
 t. Pioneer Works does not provide housing\, travel\, or visa sponsorship. 
 ELIGIBILITY: open locally\, nationally\, and internationally\; collectives
  may apply (resources shared)\; current students and programme alumni are 
 not eligible. REVIEW PROCESS: phase one is internal scoring on technical a
 bility\, uniqueness of vision\, and future potential (nontraditional caree
 r paths given special consideration)\; highest scorers advance to an exter
 nal jury of art professionals and alumni who assess diversity of demograph
 ics and media\, genuine studio need\, and fit with Pioneer Works' public-f
 acing culture. Jury convenes September 2026\; all applicants notified by l
 ate September. Supported by Dalio Philanthropies\, the Andy Warhol Foundat
 ion\, NYC Department of Cultural Affairs\, New York State Council on the A
 rts\, and the National Endowment for the Arts. APPLY: via Pioneer Works we
 bsite by 17 July 2026 (extended). Questions: residency@pioneerworks.org.\n
 \nOrganisation: Pioneer Works\nLocation: Pioneer Works\, Red Hook\, Brookl
 yn\, New York\, USA\nAward: Visual Arts: USD 5\,000 one-time award (USD 2\
 ,500 honorarium + USD 500/month stipend for 5 months). Music: USD 3\,000 o
 ne-time award (USD 2\,500 honorarium + USD 500/month stipend for 1 month).
  Free studio space and facilities access. No housing or travel provided. N
 o application fee.\nDuration: Visual Arts: 5 months. Music: 1 month. Cohor
 t begins after jury decisions in late September 2026.\n\nMore: https://pio
 neerworks.org/residency?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://pioneerworks.org/residency?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Pioneer Works\, Red Hook\, Brooklyn\, New York\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ucross-general-residency-spring-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211015Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260715
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260716
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ucross General Residency (All Disciplines): Spring 2027
DESCRIPTION:Ucross offers fully funded artist residencies on a working catt
 le ranch in the foothills of Wyoming's Bighorn Mountains\, providing unint
 errupted time and space free from the pressures and distractions of daily 
 life. Founded in 1981 with its first residency period in 1983\, Ucross hos
 ts approximately 115 individuals per year. Residencies range from two to s
 ix weeks\; at any one time up to ten people are in residence\, typically f
 our writers\, four visual artists\, and two composers. Each artist receive
 s living accommodations\, meals\, and work space at no charge. Lunches and
  dinners are prepared Monday to Friday by a professional chef (lunches del
 ivered to studios\, group dinners at 6 p.m.)\, with ample provisions for b
 reakfasts and weekends\; towels\, linens\, and weekly housekeeping are pro
 vided\, and there is cell service and wireless internet throughout. Reside
 nts provide their own working materials and arrange their own travel to Sh
 eridan\, Wyoming. The program is open to visual artists\, writers\, compos
 ers\, choreographers\, interdisciplinary artists\, performance artists\, a
 nd collaborative teams\; applicants must exhibit professional standing in 
 their field\, and both established and emerging artists are encouraged to 
 apply. Applications are submitted by discipline category: dance\, music/so
 und\, performance\, film/video\, playwriting/screenwriting\, visual art\, 
 fiction\, nonfiction\, poetry\, or interdisciplinary. The work sample and 
 project proposal are the most significant features of the application and 
 should correspond to one another\; if your work is in a single discipline\
 , select the category that best fits your project\, and apply under interd
 isciplinary only if the project genuinely combines multiple disciplines. W
 ork-sample requirements by category: DANCE\, 2-3 video files (each up to 8
  minutes and 200 MB\, or up to 3 Vimeo links\, with a timestamp if longer 
 than 8 minutes)\; MUSIC/SOUND\, 3-5 audio files (each up to 8 minutes and 
 200 MB\, or up to 3 Vimeo links\; musical scores welcome)\; PERFORMANCE an
 d FILM/VIDEO\, 1-3 video files (each up to 5 minutes and under 200 MB\, or
  up to 3 Vimeo/YouTube links\, with a timestamp if longer than 5 minutes)\
 ; PLAYWRITING/SCREENWRITING\, one complete play double-spaced noting the 2
 0-page section to read\; VISUAL ART\, 10 images at 4x6 inches and 300 dpi 
 (JPG or TIF\, no PDFs) plus a data sheet listing title\, medium\, size\, a
 nd year\; FICTION and NONFICTION\, 20 pages of double-spaced writing\; POE
 TRY\, 6-10 poems (10 maximum) in one PDF\, each poem starting on a new pag
 e\, total not exceeding 15 pages\; INTERDISCIPLINARY\, samples representin
 g the genres you plan to work in\, combining any of the above. All written
  work samples must be in English. There is a USD 40 application fee. The o
 pen call for the Spring 2027 session is open May 1 to July 15\, 2026 (appl
 y by 11:59 p.m. MT on July 15). Dedicated Fellowships for Native American 
 Visual Artists and Writers and a separate Culinary Residency are also offe
 red.\n\nOrganisation: Ucross Foundation\nLocation: Sheridan\, Wyoming\, US
 A\nAward: No charge for the residency\; living accommodations\, meals\, an
 d work space provided. USD 40 application fee.\nDuration: Spring session (
 February through early June 2027)\; residencies range from two to six week
 s\n\nMore: https://www.ucrossfoundation.org/residency-program.html?utm_sou
 rce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.ucrossfoundation.org/residency-program.html?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Sheridan\, Wyoming\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ucross-fellowship-native-american-writers-spring-2027@artificialnouveau
 .github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211015Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260715
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260716
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ucross Fellowship for Native American Writers: Spring 202
 7
DESCRIPTION:The Ucross Fellowship for Native American Writers is a dedicate
 d fellowship within Ucross Foundation's residency program\, offering fully
  funded time and space to write on a working cattle ranch in the foothills
  of Wyoming's Bighorn Mountains. The fellowship provides living accommodat
 ions\, meals\, and work space at no charge\, with no application fee. One 
 Fellowship winner is selected per session\; all applicants also have the o
 ption of being considered for a general Ucross residency. ELIGIBILITY: ope
 n to Native American writers who are practicing contemporary writers curre
 ntly producing work in one or more genres\, including but not limited to f
 iction\, nonfiction\, poetry\, drama\, screenwriting\, playwriting\, and h
 ybrid forms\; and who are enrolled members of a state-recognized or federa
 lly-recognized Tribe\, Pueblo\, Nation\, Native Community\, Political Enti
 ty\, or Alaskan Native Village. The work sample and project description ar
 e the most significant features of the application. Unless the work is gen
 uinely interdisciplinary (the genres interconnect)\, applicants are encour
 aged to apply in a single primary discipline and submit a work sample and 
 project description emphasizing that discipline. Work-sample requirements:
  FICTION\, 20 pages of double-spaced fiction (a novel excerpt\, a story\, 
 several stories\, or a combination)\; NONFICTION\, 20 pages of double-spac
 ed nonfiction\; POETRY\, 10 pages of poetry (may be single-spaced)\; PLAYW
 RITING\, one complete play (production documentation may be included if re
 levant)\, noting the 20 pages for reviewers to read\; SCREENWRITING\, one 
 complete screenplay (production documentation may be included if relevant)
 \, noting the 20 pages for reviewers to read. All writing samples should i
 nclude your full name. The open call for the Spring 2027 session closes Ju
 ly 15\, 2026 (apply by 11:59 p.m. MT). A separate Ucross Fellowship for Na
 tive American Visual Artists is also offered.\n\nOrganisation: Ucross Foun
 dation\nLocation: Sheridan\, Wyoming\, USA\nAward: Fully funded fellowship
  residency\; living accommodations\, meals\, and work space provided. No a
 pplication fee.\nDuration: Spring session (February through early June 202
 7)\; residencies range from two to six weeks\n\nMore: https://www.ucrossfo
 undation.org/residency-program.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.ucrossfoundation.org/residency-program.html?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Sheridan\, Wyoming\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ucross-fellowship-native-american-visual-artists-spring-2027@artificial
 nouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211015Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260715
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260716
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ucross Fellowship for Native American Visual Artists: Spr
 ing 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Ucross Fellowship for Native American Visual Artists is a d
 edicated fellowship within Ucross Foundation's residency program\, offerin
 g fully funded time and space to make work on a working cattle ranch in th
 e foothills of Wyoming's Bighorn Mountains. The fellowship provides living
  accommodations\, meals\, and work space at no charge\, with no applicatio
 n fee. One Fellowship winner is selected per session\; all applicants also
  have the option of being considered for a general Ucross residency. ELIGI
 BILITY: open to Native American visual artists who are practicing contempo
 rary artists currently producing work in any visual medium\, including but
  not limited to painting\, drawing\, mixed media\, sculpture\, printmaking
 \, photography\, video\, animation\, performance art\, installation\, and 
 ceramics\; and who are enrolled members of a state-recognized or federally
 -recognized Tribe\, Pueblo\, Nation\, Native Community\, Political Entity\
 , or Alaskan Native Village. The work sample and project description are t
 he most significant features of the application. Unless the work is interd
 isciplinary\, applicants are encouraged to apply in a single primary disci
 pline and submit a work sample and project description emphasizing that di
 scipline. Work-sample requirements: VISUAL ARTWORK\, submit 10 images (JPE
 G format\, no PDFs) with file names including title and size\, plus a data
  sheet listing title\, medium\, size\, and year of completion for each ima
 ge\; FILM/VIDEO/ANIMATION/PERFORMANCE\, submit up to 3 video files (each u
 nder 8 minutes and smaller than 200 MB) or up to 3 Vimeo or YouTube links.
  The open call for the Spring 2027 session closes July 15\, 2026 (apply by
  11:59 p.m. MT). A separate Ucross Fellowship for Native American Writers 
 is also offered.\n\nOrganisation: Ucross Foundation\nLocation: Sheridan\, 
 Wyoming\, USA\nAward: Fully funded fellowship residency\; living accommoda
 tions\, meals\, and work space provided. No application fee.\nDuration: Sp
 ring session (February through early June 2027)\; residencies range from t
 wo to six weeks\n\nMore: https://www.ucrossfoundation.org/residency-progra
 m.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.ucrossfoundation.org/residency-program.html?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Sheridan\, Wyoming\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:khn-center-residency-fall-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211015Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: KHN Center for the Arts: Artist Residency
DESCRIPTION:The Kimmel Harding Nelson Center for the Arts (KHN) in Nebraska
  City supports established and emerging writers\, visual artists\, and com
 posers/musicians by providing living and studio space that allows uninterr
 upted time for work\, reflection\, and creative growth. Residencies run 2 
 to 8 weeks and include free living and studio space plus a USD 175/week st
 ipend. KHN awards approximately 30-38 residencies per session\, of which r
 oughly 8-12 go to visual artists working in various media. Categories: Wri
 ters & Poets\, Visual Artists\, Composers & Musicians\, and Interdisciplin
 ary Artists. Visual artists work in one of three studios (two approximatel
 y 425 sq ft and one 258 sq ft) outfitted with work tables\, running water\
 , and storage shelves\; donated hand tools\, a bandsaw\, miter saw\, and p
 ainting easels are available. Upon request\, artists with previous printin
 g experience may access the onsite Harry Duncan Letterpress Studio (SP15 V
 andercook Proof Press\, a 16x12 inch boxcar base for adhesive plates\, fur
 niture\, fonts\, and a small inventory of printmaking tools). Open interna
 tionally. Applications are reviewed twice a year (deadlines 1 March and 1 
 September)\; the 2027 1st Session (deadline 1 September 2026) is for award
 s scheduled 4 January to 25 June 2027. All applications must be submitted 
 online via SlideRoom\; mailed or emailed applications are not considered. 
 A $35 application fee is collected through the portal.\n\nOrganisation: Ki
 mmel Harding Nelson Center for the Arts\nLocation: Nebraska City\, Nebrask
 a\, United States. Open internationally.\nAward: USD 175/week stipend\, pl
 us free living and studio space. $35 application fee\, collected via the S
 lideRoom application portal.\nDuration: Residencies of 2-8 weeks\; applica
 tions reviewed in two annual cycles (1 March and 1 September). The 2027 1s
 t Session covers awards scheduled 4 January to 25 June 2027.\n\nMore: http
 s://www.khncenterforthearts.org/residencyresident-application/how-apply?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.khncenterforthearts.org/residencyresident-application/how-a
 pply?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Nebraska City\, Nebraska\, United States. Open internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:adrianna-newell-fellowship-artsrec-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211015Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Adrianna Newell Fellowship at Arts&Rec 2026
DESCRIPTION:Inaugural Adrianna Newell Fellowship\, a live/work studio resid
 ency in Stamford\, NY (Western Catskills) for October 2026\, with possible
  extension into November. Named for board member Adrianna Newell\, it invi
 tes queer lens-based artists working in photography and moving image with 
 an active\, research-driven or conceptually rich practice\, especially wor
 k engaging queer perspectives on land\, rurality and regional space\, or q
 ueer identity\, memory\, intimacy\, community and embodiment. Open to arti
 sts from everywhere. Fellows receive a 1000 sq ft studio with attached apa
 rtment\, a $1\,500 combined materials stipend and per diem\, and introduct
 ions to the local creative community\; an open-studio presentation\, artis
 t talk or public exhibition at the end is required. Travel\, especially in
 ternational\, is not funded (residents are encouraged to seek travel suppo
 rt elsewhere). Apply by emailing a single PDF (one-page description of you
 r work and planned project\, 10 to 20 work samples with short descriptions
 \, and a current CV) to programs@artsrec.org. Deadline: 31 July 2026\; not
 ices by end of August.\n\nOrganisation: Arts&Rec Inc\nLocation: Stamford\,
  NY (Western Catskills)\, USA\nAward: Live/work residency: use of a 1000 s
 q ft studio and attached apartment\, plus a $1\,500 total materials stipen
 d and per diem. Travel (especially international) is not funded. No applic
 ation fee.\nDuration: October 2026 (four weeks to two months\, based on pr
 oject needs\; possible extension into November)\n\nMore: https://www.nyfa.
 org/opportunities/opportunity-info/?title=call-for-applications%3A-the-adr
 ianna-newell-fellowship-at-arts%26rec%2C-october-2026&id=013e03ad-6edf-47f
 b-8d14-291bdc930a31&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
URL:https://www.nyfa.org/opportunities/opportunity-info/?title=call-for-app
 lications%3A-the-adrianna-newell-fellowship-at-arts%26rec%2C-october-2026&
 id=013e03ad-6edf-47fb-8d14-291bdc930a31&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Stamford\, NY (Western Catskills)\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bloedel-reserve-creative-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211015Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Creative Residency at Bloedel Reserve: Class of 2027
DESCRIPTION:Bloedel Reserve's residency programme exists to foster creative
  thinking inspired by nature and to explore the connection between humans 
 and the environment. It is open to professional and emerging artists\, com
 posers\, writers\, botanists\, landscape architects\, researchers and othe
 rs working on themes of nature and human connection to it. Residents get a
  three-week stay at a 140-acre internationally recognised public garden an
 d forest preserve\, housed in a small home hidden in the woods off the mai
 n paths. Note for non-US applicants: the funding is structured as expense 
 reimbursement rather than a stipend\, capped at USD 1\,000\, which for tra
 nsatlantic applicants is unlikely to cover airfare plus meals over three w
 eeks.\n\nOrganisation: Bloedel Reserve\nLocation: Bainbridge Island\, Wash
 ington\, USA\, between Seattle and the Kitsap Peninsula\, reachable by fer
 ry from Seattle or by car via the Kitsap Peninsula. Residents must be 21 o
 r older. International applicants are eligible but are funded differently 
 from domestic ones (see amount).\nAward: USD 22 application fee. Furnished
  private house on the grounds with linens\, stocked kitchen\, wi-fi\, laun
 dry and use of a smart car\; an electric piano is available. Domestic resi
 dents receive a USD 1\,000 stipend. International residents receive no sti
 pend and may instead submit up to USD 1\,000 of expenses for reimbursement
 . Residents pay for their own meals and their own travel to Bainbridge Isl
 and. There is no dedicated studio\; residents work in the house.\nDuration
 : Three weeks\; residents stay one at a time with no overlap\n\nMore: http
 s://bloedelreserve.slideroom.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral#/login/program/88209
URL:https://bloedelreserve.slideroom.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral#/login/program/88209
LOCATION:Bainbridge Island\, Washington\, USA\, between Seattle and the Kit
 sap Peninsula\, reachable by ferry from Seattle or by car via the Kitsap P
 eninsula. Residents must be 21 or older. International applicants are elig
 ible but are funded differently from domestic ones (see amount).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kluge-fellowships-library-of-congress-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211015Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kluge Fellowships at the Library of Congress 2027
DESCRIPTION:The John W. Kluge Center awards twelve residential fellowships 
 a year for humanistic and social science research that draws on the Librar
 y of Congress collections\, with interdisciplinary and cross-cultural proj
 ects particularly welcome. Fellows get four to eleven months in residence 
 at a stipend of USD 5\,000 per month\, work and discussion space in the Je
 fferson Building\, and access to the Library's specialist staff\, the worl
 d's largest law library\, extensive multilingual books and periodicals\, a
 nd deep special collections of manuscripts\, maps\, music\, films\, record
 ed sound\, prints and photographs\, alongside the wider scholarly communit
 y in Washington. The Center was established in 2000 through a USD 60 milli
 on endowment from John W. Kluge. ELIGIBILITY: scholars who received a term
 inal advanced degree (the highest degree available in the discipline\, usu
 ally a PhD\, JD or international equivalent) within the past seven years\,
  in the humanities\, social sciences\, or a professional field such as arc
 hitecture or law. The degree must be in hand at the time of submission\, a
 nd full-time students are therefore out of scope. Open to U.S. citizens an
 d foreign nationals\; selected foreign nationals are assisted in obtaining
  a Library-sponsored J-1 visa\, though holders of H-1B visas are not eligi
 ble. APPLICATION: through the Library's online portal\, opening 15 April 2
 026 and closing 11:59pm on 15 September 2026. Materials required are an ap
 plication form in English\, a CV (max 2 pages)\, a 500-word research abstr
 act\, a one-page statement on how the Library's collections will serve the
  work\, a project proposal with work plan (max 3 pages)\, a bibliography (
 max 3 pages)\, and two reference letters from referees who have read the p
 roposal. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: John W. Kluge Center\, Library of Congre
 ss\nLocation: Washington\, DC\, United States (in residence at the Thomas 
 Jefferson Building\, 101 Independence Ave SE). Open to applicants of any n
 ationality: U.S. citizens and foreign nationals alike\, with J-1 exchange 
 visitor visa sponsorship provided for those who need it.\nAward: Stipend o
 f USD 5\,000 per month for a residency of four to eleven months\, so USD 2
 0\,000 to USD 55\,000 depending on the tenure awarded. Twelve Kluge Fellow
 ships are awarded each year. Stipends are paid monthly by electronic trans
 fer to a U.S. bank account. No application fee\, no participation fee.\nDu
 ration: Four to eleven months in residence\; fellows may take up residence
  at any point in the 18-month window after receiving the fellowship letter
 \n\nMore: https://www.loc.gov/programs/john-w-kluge-center/chairs-fellowsh
 ips/fellowships/kluge-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.loc.gov/programs/john-w-kluge-center/chairs-fellowships/fel
 lowships/kluge-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, DC\, United States (in residence at the Thomas Jeffer
 son Building\, 101 Independence Ave SE). Open to applicants of any nationa
 lity: U.S. citizens and foreign nationals alike\, with J-1 exchange visito
 r visa sponsorship provided for those who need it.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-public-entrepreneur-in-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211015Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: New_ Public Entrepreneur in Residence\, Pilot Cohort
DESCRIPTION:New_ Public is piloting a virtual Entrepreneur in Residence pro
 gramme for solo founders building alternatives to the mainstream social pl
 atforms. Its framing: people are turning away from algorithmic social medi
 a\, AI bots are taking over the big platforms\, and users are retreating i
 nto private closed spaces\, which leaves an opening for builders working o
 n a new information ecosystem rather than the status quo. The residency su
 pplies research\, experts\, connections and tools\; the founder brings the
  vision and experience. TWO FOCUS AREAS: humans connecting to humans\, mea
 ning products that bring people into meaningful contact online (forums\, m
 essaging apps\, groups\, microblogging\, in-person meetups)\, including AI
 -powered platforms and tools as long as they connect people to people rath
 er than people to bots\; and local and civic information environments\, me
 aning tools for distributing local news\, building community knowledge and
  facilitating collective sensemaking\, so people can get trustworthy infor
 mation about their communities. WHAT RESIDENTS DO: use self-directed time 
 to prototype ideas\, test assumptions and gain clarity\; participate in re
 gular check-ins\, engage with the other resident and the wider team\, and 
 take candid feedback on product and strategy\; engage seriously with go-to
 -market questions about who the user is\, how they find you\, and the path
  to sustainability\; and help shape the programme itself\, since the pilot
  cohort's feedback directly informs how the EIR programme runs going forwa
 rd. WHO THEY WANT: entrepreneurial founders ready to build their next thin
 g within the focus areas\, with a track record of or clear drive toward wo
 rk that builds social trust\, connection and belonging\; strong product th
 inkers with good judgment\, a relentless focus on user needs\, and opennes
 s to coaching on mission-aligned business models. An early idea is enough\
 , no working product required. The pilot is open to founders with somethin
 g else going on alongside\, though the full programme will most likely be 
 full time. APPLICATION PROCESS: submit the written application online\; sh
 ortlisted applicants submit a short video by 18 September 2026 covering ba
 ckground\, motivation and a quick pitch\; full applications and videos go 
 to the advisory board\, with offers made in early October ahead of the kic
 koff. FUTURE COHORTS: New_ Public plans to open the next cohort to the UK 
 and EU from spring 2027. Deadline 11:59pm Pacific on Wednesday 9 September
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: New_ Public\nLocation: Remote\, with an in-person 
 kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (19 to 22 October 2026). Open to app
 licants based in the United States or Canada.\nAward: Stipend of USD 22\,5
 00\, paid in three monthly installments across the residency\, each contin
 gent on continued active participation. Plus USD 1\,500 toward an AI tool 
 of the resident's choice for coding\, prototyping or design. Two residents
  are selected in the pilot cohort. Also provided: hands-on product and str
 ategy support from senior New_ Public staff and outside experts\; access t
 o New_ Public's five years of original research on moderation\, governance
 \, community stewardship and platform design\, plus direct access to the r
 esearchers behind it\; and introductions across New_ Public's network of m
 ajor platforms\, startups\, university researchers and funders for hiring\
 , fundraising\, early user testing and community partnerships. Residents m
 ust submit a Form W-9 or W-8BEN to receive the stipend. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Three months\, from the week of 19 October 2026 to 29 January
  2027\, run remotely apart from the opening retreat\n\nMore: https://newpu
 blic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://newpublic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with an in-person kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (
 19 to 22 October 2026). Open to applicants based in the United States or C
 anada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mats-residency-program-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211015Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: MATS Residency Program: 6-24 Month Funded AI Safety Resea
 rch (Winter 2027 intake)
DESCRIPTION:The MATS Residency\, announced in August 2026 as a new track al
 ongside the seasonal MATS fellowship\, funds 6 to 24 months of high-indepe
 ndence AI safety research for experienced researchers\, whether already in
  AI safety or moving in from adjacent fields. COMPENSATION: USD 155\,000 t
 o 285\,000 per year gross depending on location\, seniority and experience
 \, plus insurance\, retirement plan\, PTO (17 days US / 20 days UK)\, conf
 erence funding\, catered meals\, and up to USD 25\,000 in relocation suppo
 rt. ELIGIBILITY: at least one substantial piece of research-caliber work\;
  credentials are not required. Full-time (part-time case by case for acade
 mics on sabbatical)\, in person at MATS offices in Berkeley\, London\, or 
 Washington DC\, with visa support where required. TIMELINE: the Winter 202
 7 intake window runs 25 August to 31 October 2026 (AoE)\, with decisions b
 y end of November and interviews plus a paid work test in the first week o
 f December\; three intake windows per year.\n\nOrganisation: MATS (ML Alig
 nment and Theory Scholars)\nLocation: In-person at MATS offices in Berkele
 y\, London\, or Washington DC\; visa support provided where required. For 
 experienced researchers with at least one substantial piece of research-ca
 liber work.\nAward: USD 155\,000 to 285\,000 per year gross (set by locati
 on\, seniority\, and experience)\, plus medical/dental/vision/life insuran
 ce\, retirement plan\, paid time off\, conference funding\, catered meals\
 , and up to USD 25\,000 relocation support. No application fee.\nDuration:
  6 to 24 months with rolling start dates\; three intake windows per year.\
 n\nMore: https://www.matsprogram.org/residency?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.matsprogram.org/residency?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In-person at MATS offices in Berkeley\, London\, or Washington DC\
 ; visa support provided where required. For experienced researchers with a
 t least one substantial piece of research-caliber work.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
